    "href": "/developers/contribution-checklist/",
    "searchText": "prepare a contribution scope, implement, verify, review, and hand off a proctor change while preserving unrelated work, licensing, and source authority. prepare a contribution this checklist covers the repository-owned technical path from an understood change to a reviewable diff. it does not invent project governance that has not yet been decided. 1. establish the scope - state the user, operator, developer, or system outcome. - identify the domain terms and invariants involved. - find the current implementation, tests, nearest component contract, and relevant public maturity statement. - inspect git status and distinguish existing work from your change. - list affected public contracts, generated artifacts, and documentation authorities before editing. - stop and request a decision when missing information would materially change behavior, security, compatibility, licensing, or deployment support. 2. choose the smallest complete slice a complete slice crosses every boundary needed for one behavior and no more. it may include a model transition, application use case, store operation, http mapping, openapi source, tests, and public guidance together. it should not include: - placeholder packages or interfaces; - broad refactors justified only by symmetry or file size; - a second source of truth for an existing contract; - unrelated formatting or generated noise; or - speculative support for an unselected platform, provider, or workflow. 3. implement through the owner follow the applicable workflow: - server behavior; - hosted webapp; - reusable module; - http and openapi; - persistence; or - documentation. update source and tests together. when a behavior or authority changes, update its component contract, public guide and maturity, api source, or owning tracked reference in the same slice. 4. handle upstream work correctly mattermost is an eligible behavior and source reference, not an automatic template. before copying or substantially adapting upstream expression: 1. record the exact repository, revision, path, and governing license; 2. confirm the license is compatible with the destination; 3. rework the behavior through proctor's domain, architecture, security, and accessibility boundaries; 4. preserve required notices and record provenance immediately; and 5. review for copied branding, fixtures, secrets, and assumptions. reusable apache-2.0 modules cannot contain implementation copied from agpl, source-available, or commercial mattermost code. mattermost-derived server work must be recorded in server/notice when required. 5. verify in increasing scope run the focused test while developing, then the owning module gate, the relevant integration target, and the highest honest product gate. use the testing guide instead of running every expensive suite after every edit. at minimum before handoff: for server or documentation boundary changes: 6. review the diff as a reviewer - read the complete diff, including generated artifacts. - confirm generated files match their human-owned inputs. - search for credentials, tokens, private keys, real email addresses, student data, exam answers, local paths, hostnames, and oversized fixtures. - check that errors, logs, metrics, audit values, and examples remain bounded. - confirm unrelated user work was neither staged nor reformatted. - re-read changed prose for promises not proved by released behavior. - verify that removed authority was actually removed rather than left as a stale compatibility copy. 7. hand off clearly report: 1. the outcome, not only the files changed; 2. the durable boundary or contract that now owns it; 3. the focused and broad checks that passed; 4. any integration or environment-dependent check not run; and 5. every unresolved decision or source todo that remains. do not describe a preview behavior as generally available, a best-effort effect as durable, an integrity flag as a finding of guilt, or an inferred platform as supported."
