    "href": "/developers/development-configuration/",
    "searchText": "configure the development runtime override local ports, origins, images, and generated state without editing tracked topology files or committing secrets. configure the development runtime the root makefile is the supported configuration boundary for local product development. use its variables to avoid port conflicts or to test a controlled image upgrade. do not edit compose files, generated json, or generated secrets for a personal workstation preference. know the precedence for the root make lifecycle, the highest-precedence value wins: 1. a command-line make value; 2. an assignment in the ignored root config.override.mk ; 3. an environment value; then 4. the tracked default in build/make/config.mk . use the command line for one experiment: use the environment when a parent tool owns the value: use config.override.mk for a stable workstation preference: config.override.mk is ignored by git. keep it local. never place production credentials or a shared environment's connection details in it. keep the listener and public origin distinct proctor server port selects the loopback listener. the default proctor server public url is http://localhost: followed by that port. the public url is the canonical browser origin used for redirects, browser transactions, and local mail action links. for the compiled hosted webapp, overriding only the port is enough: for an origin-sensitive vite flow, the public url must instead match vite: localhost and 127.0.0.1 are both loopback, but browsers treat them as different origins. use the exact configured scheme, host, and port throughout one flow. local http action links are accepted only for an explicitly loopback public origin. a non-loopback http public url remains invalid; production origins use https. common port and project overrides make variable default consumer --- --- --- proctor compose project name proctor-dev compose network, containers, and named volumes proctor server port 8065 single-node http listener and seed api address proctor server public url http://localhost:8065 canonical single-node browser origin proctor metrics port 8067 protected proctor metrics listener proctor cluster http port 18065 haproxy cluster gateway proctor postgres port 15432 postgresql host binding proctor redis port 16379 redis host binding proctor minio port 19000 minio s3 api proctor minio console port 19001 minio console proctor mailpit smtp port 11025 mailpit smtp listener proctor mailpit http port 18025 mailpit browser and api proctor prometheus port 19090 prometheus browser and api proctor grafana port 13000 grafana browser and api proctor loki port 13100 loki api proctor otel health port 13133 collector health extension when you change a service port through make, the generated server configuration, compose binding, and relevant test address receive the same value. if a custom tool bypasses make, it owns that coordination itself. use a different proctor compose project name when two repository checkouts must run simultaneously. also assign distinct host ports; project names isolate containers and volumes, not host listeners. controlled image overrides tracked defaults select exact service versions and immutable multi-platform digests. the corresponding variables are: override an image only for an intentional compatibility or upgrade test. record the exact tag and digest in review evidence. a mutable tag alone does not prove which artifact passed. understand generated .build/dev state make dev-state recreates derived configuration while preserving existing generated secrets. the default ignored tree contains: path meaning safe to commit? --- --- --- .build/dev/config/ full generated single-node and per-cluster-node server configuration no .build/dev/secrets/environment bootstrap, sealing, and other development secret values no .build/dev/secrets/metrics-token prometheus bearer credential no .build/dev/secrets/metrics- .pem local metrics certificate and private key no .build/dev/observability/ generated prometheus target discovery no .build/dev/logs/ single-node and per-node structured logs no .build/dev/prometheus/ local time-series history no .build/dev/otel/ collector file-read checkpoints no .build/dev/seed/credentials.json random synthetic account credentials, mode 0600 never .build/dev/seed/fixture.json synthetic fixture identifiers, mode 0600 no these files are outputs, not documentation or configuration authority. change the tracked server example, build scripts, make variables, or topology owner that generates them. a direct edit can disappear on the next lifecycle run and cannot be reviewed reliably. preserve or recreate secrets intentionally generated secret creation is idempotent: ordinary make run-server , make run-cluster , and make dev-state runs reuse existing values. this keeps encrypted local payloads and protected metrics readable across restarts. make dev-down stops containers and preserves generated state. make dev-reset deletes compose named volumes and guarded seed files, but it does not present itself as a general-purpose deletion command for all ignored build output. treat deliberate secret rotation or removal as a separate local maintenance decision; stale encrypted payloads become unreadable when their key is removed. diagnose an override before changing code when an override appears ineffective: 1. check whether the same variable was supplied on the make command line; 2. inspect config.override.mk for a higher-precedence local assignment; 3. check the exported environment; 4. confirm the spelling against make help and build/make/config.mk ; 5. restart the process or containers that consume immutable startup configuration; and 6. verify the resulting listener or generated file without printing secrets. avoid copying all of .build/dev/secrets/environment into a terminal or issue. inspect only the non-secret field needed for diagnosis, and redact values before sharing output. return to local setup for the first-success sequence or continue to cluster development for the three-node topology."
